# Line endings, settled 2026-09-06.
#
# Until this file existed the repository had no opinion, so core.autocrlf on
# each machine had the last word. On this one it is true, which means git wrote
# CRLF into the working tree on checkout and normalised it back to LF on read.
# Both halves of that are invisible: every file in the index was already LF, and
# a CRLF working file never once showed up as modified in `git status`.
#
# What it cost, measured the day this landed. `gofmt -l internal desktop cmd`
# named 247 files. Stripping CR from a copy of each and re-running it left 28 —
# so 219 of those complaints were line endings, and the tool that reports real
# formatting drift was reporting nine parts noise to one part signal. Nobody
# reads a list like that, which is how 24 genuinely misformatted files reached
# main. Fourteen of the twenty-eight were themselves CRLF, so the noise was not
# merely loud, it was hiding half the signal inside itself.
#
# eol=lf rather than eol=native on purpose: the whole tree is Go, TypeScript and
# Svelte, every toolchain over it writes LF, and "whatever this machine prefers"
# is the setting that produced the mess above.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Named rather than left to text=auto, so that a future rule cannot widen its
# way over them. auto already treats these as binary; this says so out loud.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.gz binary
*.mp3 binary
*.woff2 binary
*.lockb binary

# desktop/wails-dev.bat is deliberately NOT pinned to CRLF. Batch files usually
# want it, that one has been LF in both the index and the working tree all
# along, and it runs — so the rule that would "fix" it is the only thing here
# that would change a working file's behaviour.
#
# One more trap worth writing down: Artifacts.svelte and BackgroundWork.svelte
# are detected as binary, because they carry bytes no text file should. text=auto
# leaves them alone and must keep doing so. A blanket `*.svelte text` added later
# would rewrite them, which is why there is no such line here.
